<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Poggio Bracciolini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1380">1380</a>–1459</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Gian Francesco Poggio Bracciolini, Poggio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Poggio Bracciolini was an Italian scholar and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanist who served seven popes in the Roman Curia, rising under Martin V to papal secretary. While attending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> he searched the libraries of Swiss and Swabian abbeys, and between 1415 and 1417 recovered many lost classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> works, among them De rerum natura by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretius">Lucretius</a>, De architectura by Vitruvius, Quintilian's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/institutio-oratoria">Institutio Oratoria</a> and orations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>. He was one of the most prolific scribes of the new Humanist minuscule. In 1453 he became Chancellor of the Florentine Republic, and he died in 1459 before finishing his history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/poggio-bracciolini.jpg" alt="Poggio Bracciolini" width="330" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio Luciani (Italian printmaker; fl. 1700–1738) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gianfrancesco_Poggio_Bracciolini_-_Imagines_philologorum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Classical Latin texts were preserved through the Middle Ages largely by copying in monasteries and cathedral schools, and the recovery of lost works by Renaissance scholars, such as <b>Poggio Bracciolini</b>’s discovery of Lucretius in 1417, helped shape modern thought.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The roman type of most printed books today descends from a handwriting that <b>Poggio Bracciolini</b> and his Florentine circle developed around 1400, copying the Carolingian script of the old manuscripts in which they found the classics.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Terranuova Bracciolini, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.55,11.59&amp;year=1431" aria-label="Terranuova Bracciolini on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4829 619 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Poggio Bracciolini"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5279 889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5279 889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.55,11.59&amp;year=1431">What was here in 1431? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
